 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>JOC peels the tails off too and tucks the shrimp in tail down (“you can show the tail side up if you like”).</li><li>We keep the tails on so the red tail pokes out of the top — the way Nagoya shops sell them, and the fun part.</li><li>Peeled tails-off works exactly the same.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Tails on and up (our change)"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Match the rice to the shrimp: smaller shrimp, smaller rice balls.</li><li>Very big shrimp can be cut in half (tails off, then).</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Shrimp size"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>JOC: there’s no alternative to deep-frying for real tenmusu.</li><li>The thick batter is the point — it drinks up the mentsuyu and that’s where the flavour comes from.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "No shortcut on the frying"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Line a small bowl with plastic wrap, add rice and a sprinkle of salt, make the well, add the tempura, pull the wrap up around it and shape the triangle through the wrap.</li><li>Unwrap, then add the nori.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Without touching the rice"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Not eating them right away?</li><li>Keep the nori strips in an airtight bag and wrap them on just before eating.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Crisp nori"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Born in the 1930s at Senju, a tempura restaurant in Tsu (Mie): the owner made rice balls with shrimp tempura as a quick lunch for her husband.</li><li>It reached Nagoya in 1980 and became one of its signature foods.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Where it’s from"
  }
 ],
